That has been my (admittedly limited) experience as well.
Titans seem to constrain themselves mostly to doing one
of three things:  throwing boulders at you (if boulders are
available), attempting to harm you directly, either by
melee or by cursing your items (if they're within melee
range), or summoning nasties.  Liches appear to have a
somewhat more varied repertoire, from what little I have
seen of them.  (Admittedly, I usually nuke them with a
blessed scroll first chance I get, so I haven't seen them
that much, and my experience may be skewed by the
small sample size.)  Consequently, they spend less
time doing any given thing -- such as summoning.

> I think the liches are actually weaker *because* they
> have tactical teleport.

Perhaps so -- although closing to melee range with
a lich isn't always what you really want to do.

> When encountering a titan, you need to spend turns
> approaching him, and during that time most of his
> spells besides summoning aren't applicable.
> Worse, a cloud of nasties can stall you from reaching
> adjacency, giving him time to do it again.

Yes, I've noticed that.  Getting close enough to a
Titan to kill it with melee can be a very hit-or-miss
proposition.  Even ranged attacks require you to get
directly lined up, which isn't always easy, because
they tend to keep you pretty well surrounded much
of the time.  Occasionally the titan will approach you
and attempt melee, which gives you a chance to kill
it; but you may have to kill a hundred nasties or more
before that happens.  Meanwhile the titan just sort
of wanders around, summoning new nasties every
time he notices you're not quite surrounded enough,
and sometimes when you still are.  Being fast helps,
because you can often kill monsters and step into
their space before slow things (e.g., dragons) can
step in and fill up the tile.  Very fast is even better.

It's easier if you have controlled teleport and are on
a level that allows it -- but Medusa's level doesn't.

The good thing about the entry hall is that the
titan is just as confined as you are.  Also, the
walls can be used for ray bouncing, which makes
it easier to line up to hit him.  A titan on the other,
open version of Medusa's level (the one that,
fortunately, doesn't have a guaranteed titan)
would actually be worse, I think.


